_027=0 screens. These are common high-prevalence nominal binary predictors, so the nominal-level coding issue does not apply to them, but the cell imbalance and multiplicity argue against treating any one near-threshold point estimate as a secondary marker. I therefore defer rather than close the unresolved ones that remain formally compatible with clinical effects. For outcome_001, H135 through H137 test feature_034, feature_002, and feature_060 within feature_050=0, the larger complement of the dominant feature_050 marker. All signed upper bounds are well below the outcome_001 clinical threshold, so I reject each as a clinically meaningful stronger-dependency association in that stratum. H123 tests residual feature_027 association with outcome_001 within feature_050=1; R101 is near zero but the upper bound slightly exceeds the global threshold because of the small exposed cell. I leave it unresolved and deferred rather than accept or strongly reject. None of the new results meets interval-based acceptance. I request validation of H127, the sign-corrected negative feature_042 outcome_014 comparison, because R89 shows a signed estimate +0.0351 with interval -0.0012 to +0.0714: the magnitude is well above the clinical threshold and the interval is close to excluding zero, but the discovery lower bound does not clear clinical significance. This is the strongest prior candidate and warrants independent validation. I also registered a controlled feature_042 test within feature_027=0 to determine whether any validated H127 effect is independent of the dominant feature_027 outcome_014 marker. All statements remain associations within the supplied dataset and defined eligibility sets, not causal, mechanistic, or externally generalizable claims.",
